<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Please evaluate and advise on chronic pain due to trauma and post-traumatic osteoarthritis, right ankle and foot and sequelae of poliomyelitis and long term (current) use of opiate analgesic and vitamin D deficiency, unspecified. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Physical medicine and rehabilitation referral for a multimodal rehabilitation plan: exercise therapy, bracing and orthotic review, a shoe lift for the leg-length difference, and function goals. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Seen in physical medicine and rehabilitation consultation at the request of Marisol Ortega, MD. [S]</paragraph><list><item>Constant back and pelvic pain with burning in the right leg that limits how long she can stand and paint. [S]</item><item>Worried that her opioid dose will be cut suddenly and that the pain will become unbearable. [S]</item><item>Wants to keep teaching her students and painting every day. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Magdalena Carmen Frida Kahlo Calderón was born on July 6, 1907, in her parents&apos; house in Coyoacán, Mexico City, now known as the Blue House, the daughter of Guillermo Kahlo and Matilde Calderón. [D]</item><item>In 1913, at the age of six, Kahlo fell ill with polio; afterward her right leg was thinner, slightly shorter, and less developed, with the foot turned outward, and her father encouraged cycling and swimming. [D]</item><item>A neurologist&apos;s retrospective review proposes that Kahlo also had a congenital spinal anomaly (spina bifida). [R]</item><item>On September 17, 1925, at 18, Kahlo was riding a bus that was struck by a streetcar; a metal handrail impaled her through the hip and fractured her pelvis, and she had three fractures of the spinal column, a fractured collarbone, two rib fractures, a dislocated right shoulder, and eleven fractures of the right leg with a dislocated foot. [D]</item><item>While immobilized for several months after the accident, Kahlo began to paint, using an easel adapted for painting in bed. [D]</item><item>Kahlo lived with chronic pain for the rest of her life, underwent many operations, some with long convalescences and serious consequences, and used about 25 different corsets. [D]</item><item>In 1944, because of increasingly limited mobility, Kahlo had to stop attending her classes at the La Esmeralda school of painting and sculpture, and some students continued to work with her at the Blue House. [D]</item><item>Her pain has been interpreted retrospectively as including chronic neuropathic pain and central sensitization. [R]</item><item>Kahlo&apos;s father, Guillermo Kahlo, had epilepsy and died in 1941; her mother, Matilde Calderón, died in 1932. [D]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

